{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Illumina's EU IVDR table lists no NovaSeq X product, and the NovaSeq 6000Dx is the only high-throughput IVD sequencer, so EU regulated labs have no certified path off the 6000 platform.","detail":"The page (read 12 Oct 2026) lists three IVD instruments, all Class A: MiSeqDx (≤15 Gb/run), NextSeq 550Dx (≤120 Gb/run) and NovaSeq 6000Dx (up to 6 Tb/run). It names no NovaSeq X Dx, X Plus Dx or any planned high-throughput IVD instrument. The IVDR deadlines are 31 Dec 2027 (Class D), 31 Dec 2028 (Class C) and 31 Dec 2029 (Class B and sterile A). Regulated EU volume that needs a CE-IVDR instrument therefore has to stay on the 6000Dx while the X page markets the roadmap only for research use.","url":"https://www.illumina.com/clinical/diagnostics/ivdr-guidance-resources.html","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"Illumina IVDR guidance page","source_type":"other","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Adds the three-instrument table with classes and the specific Class C/D deadlines; the known list only notes the 6000Dx is the sole IVD."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Illumina's IVDR assay table has no assay with an IVDR plan for the X, and its one flagship oncology panel is still on the old IVDD CE mark.","detail":"TruSight Oncology Comprehensive (EU, Class C) is listed as 'CE mark (IVDD)' with IVDR compliance in progress. The page gives no date. The Class C transition deadline is 31 Dec 2028. The same product runs only on the NextSeq 550Dx, so oncology IVD volume in the EU sits on mid-throughput kit, not on the X. The implication for the thesis is that clinical lock-in on older platforms may last into 2028–29.","url":"https://www.illumina.com/clinical/diagnostics/ivdr-guidance-resources.html","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"Illumina IVDR guidance page","source_type":"other","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known list covers the US TSO PMA (NextSeq 550Dx only). This adds the EU IVDR status, still on IVDD."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"The NovaSeq 6000Dx's own product page lists only 6000-family kits and no assay, so the regulated tail has no named assay and no X migration path.","detail":"The page lists compatible items as the 6000Dx Library Tube, S2/S4 Reagent Kits (300-cycle) and DNA Prep with Enrichment Dx plus the DRAGEN app. The comparison table names only 'Future Illumina assays' and 'Future partner assays' for the 6000Dx, with TSO Comprehensive and VeriSeq NIPT appearing only under the NextSeq 550Dx. In the US the intended use is targeted sequencing only and is explicitly not for whole-genome sequencing. Regulated 6000Dx volume is therefore small and limited to panels, and its use cases do not include the genome work that drives clinical Gb.","url":"https://www.illumina.com/systems/ivd-instruments/novaseq-6000dx.html","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000Dx product page","source_type":"other","quote":"Not intended for whole-genome or de novo sequencing","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known list has no 6000Dx spec or intended-use detail. This shows the Dx tail is panel-only."},
